Raspberry Pi (RasPi) is widely used as a vantage point in Internet measurement infrastructure; however, its hardware constraints can introduce artifacts that undermine measurement reliability. We investigate anomalous idle periods observed during Ookla speed tests on a RasPi using cross-layer analysis combining NetLog traces with packet captures. We identify two root causes: (1) delayed generation of new HTTP transactions between object downloads, and (2) TCP zero window packets which indicate that the RasPi cannot drain its receive buffer fast enough under load. Both reduce the effective number of throughput samples in the fixed 15-second test window, potentially yielding unreliable speed estimates.